    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d77">

                <pb facs="0190" n="78" sameAs="frus:frus1907p1/pg78"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 5301/2.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to Minister
                    Wilson</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1907-04-03"><hi rendition="#italic">April 3,
                        1907</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 97.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    dispatch, No. 157, of the 7th ultimo, referring to the complaint of a company
                    dealing <pb facs="0191" n="79" xml:id="pg79"/> in American lubricating oils that
                    the Belgian Government discriminated against such products by inserting in the
                    specifications of supply bids for furnishing oils to the government railways the
                    requirement of “Russian” origin.</p>
                <p>Your correspondence with the Belgian Government, resulting in its removal of the
                    discrimination, copies of which accompany your dispatch, have been read with
                    interest; and the department desires to commend your action and to express
                    gratification at the success of your representations.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Bacon.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
